akl96","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1765040229461,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":10,"user":{"displayName":"amit yadav","userId":"00651932750270724739"}},"outputId":"4a12393a-5f29-40a2-b260-ddc05d83c4ba"},"execution_count":5,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Company: Infosys\n","Company: Infosys\n"]},{"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":["'Infosys'"],"application/vnd.google.colaboratory.intrinsic+json":{"type":"string"}},"metadata":{},"execution_count":5}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["📝 Deep Explanation (Clear & Simple):\n","What is a Class Method?\n","\n","A method that receives the class itself as the first parameter instead of the object.\n","\n","Type\tFirst parameter\tAccess to\tUse case\n","Instance method\tself\tObject data\tDefault behavior\n","Class method\tcls\tClass data\tShared state\n","Static method\tNo self or cls\tUtility functions\tIndependent logic\n","✔ How it works:\n","\n","When you call Employee.show_company(), Python passes the class (Employee) automatically as cls.\n","\n","So inside the method:\n","\n","cls.company == Employee.company\n","\n","\n","This allows:\n","\n","Reading class attributes\n","\n","Modifying class attributes\n","\n","Creating alternate constructors\n","\n","✔ Why use a class method?\n","\n","Because sometimes you want to modify or access class-level shared data rather than object-specific data.\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","Changing company name for ALL employees\n","\n","Creating objects from alternative formats (e.g., from a string)"],"metadata":{"id":"JbGm8Iryz8Bd"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["🔵 Example 6 — Empty Class + Dynamic Attribute Assignment\n","❗ This confuses many beginners — here’s the simplest explanation."],"metadata":{"id":"IaDs8cTPz-qT"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["\n","class Demo:\n","    pass # do nothing\n","\n","obj = Demo()\n","obj.name = \"Amit\"\n","obj.age = 30\n","print(obj.name, obj.age)"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"njoS_Ygb0Ap3","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1764839210655,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":21,"user":{"displayName":"amit yadav","userId":"00651932750270724739"}},"outputId":"8296af46-ee6f-45ca-b541-c4168111079c"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Amit 30\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["\n","obj1 = Demo()\n","obj1.namee = \"Amit\"\n","obj1.agee = 30\n","print(obj1.namee, obj1.agee)"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"8Jj2hFYXnOZk","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1764839222855,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":17,"user":{"displayName":"amit yadav","userId":"00651932750270724739"}},"outputId":"619204c2-7042-4de7-dd83-411daab1bc65"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Amit 30\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["\n","\n","📝 Deep Explanation:\n","✔ What is happening?\n","\n","Python allows objects to have attributes added at runtime, even if the class doesn't define them.\n","\n","The Demo class is empty — BUT Python objects have a flexible internal structure that allows:\n","\n","obj.new_attribute = value\n","\n","\n","This is called dynamic attribute binding.\n","\n","✔ Why does Python allow this?\n","\n","Because Python uses a dynamic object model.\n","\n","Every object has an internal dictionary called:\n","\n","obj.__dict__\n","\n","\n","When you do:\n","\n","obj.name = \"Amit\"\n","\n","\n","Internally Python does:\n","\n","obj.__dict__[\"name\"] = \"Amit\"\n","\n","\n","Which means:\n","\n","You can attach ANY data to ANY object\n","\n","The class doesn’t need predefined attributes\n","\n","✔ When is this useful?\n","\n","Creating flexible objects\n","\n","Prototyping\n","\n","Mock objects in testing\n","\n","Quick data containers (similar to structs in C)\n","\n","✔ Important Note:\n","\n","This works only if the class does not use __slots__."],"metadata":{"id":"5PQnZuqL0HHs"}},{"cell_type":"ma